// error: what is passed for `body` of `isolate` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `make`, a `-> Buffer[int]`, which is a by-name value, a function whose type does not say what it captures; mark the parameter `@isolating`, and what is passed for it is checked in turn
// error: what is passed for `body` of `isolate` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses the state of the actor `Keeper`, which only the actor's own code reaches; copy what it needs into a `val` first
// error: what is passed for `body` of `isolate` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `kept`, a `Buffer[int]`, which is mutable; an immutable copy (`toVector`) is sendable
// error: what is passed for `f` of `Isolated.with` may use only sendable values from around it, but it uses `kept`, a `Buffer[int]`, which is mutable; an immutable copy (`toVector`) is sendable
// error: `Buffer[int]` does not conform to `Sendable`, the bound of `R` in `with`: it is mutable; an immutable copy (`toVector`) is sendable
// An `isolate` body and a `with` function use only sendable values from around them: a buffer the
// caller keeps is refused to both, and so is the actor's own state, read without a `val` copied
// first. A `with` function gives back a sendable value, so the objects cannot leave through it. A
// parameter marked `@isolated` holds a function whose module-level reach was never checked, so it
// may not be passed on for an `@isolating` one.
import avin.actors.{isolate, spawn}

passOn(@isolated make: -> Buffer[int]) = isolate(make)

actor Keeper(size: int) receives int
  receive
    n ->
      val b = isolate
        val made = Buffer[int]()
        made.append(size)
        made
      ()

main()
  val kept = Buffer[int]()
  val a = isolate(kept)
  val b = isolate(Buffer[int]())
  b.with(x -> kept.size)
  val leaked = b.with(x -> x)
  spawn(Keeper(1))
